  19: ## Rules for every phase
  20: 
  21: 1. **Input:** Eyes v2 with zero input collisions ([revision](restart-plan-revision-2026-11-03.md), problem 1).
  22: 2. **Scoring:** every proposed move is scored from unchanged stored memory with `frozenScores` (revision, problem 4). The same single move score scores every task. There is no idea selector, no task label, no context signal and no hand-written rule that combines ideas.
  23: 3. **Memory (corrected later on 2026-11-03):** each phase declares, before data, either brain memory only or FlyChess with a disclosed engineered memory, as the researcher allowed on 2026-11-01. Results are reported for the declared system. The exact-recall store alone is always a comparison arm. An engineered-memory condition must also beat the same retrieval on raw eyes, on random or rewired features, with no memory and with mismatched memory (revision, problem 7 correction). Zero exact repeats between test and training inputs. The first version of this rule said "brain memory only"; that was a mistake.
  24: 4. **Feedback:** only the fly's own chosen move gets feedback, through the modeled dopamine cells, from an independent checker certificate. Unknown gives nothing. No feedback on test sets.
  25: 5. **Equal practice:** every arm gets the same number of feedback trials in the same order, with the same seeds. An arm that skips a training block gets the same number of **filler trials** instead: the non-chess arbitrary-pairing trials of the 2026-11-01 pilot, which carry feedback but no chess fact.
  26: 6. **Each pass score must hold in all three runs.** Every run gets an independent replay audit.
  27: 7. **Necessity:** each locked pair differs between its twins in the condition of exactly one idea. A system that lacks that idea gives both twins the same answer and gets that pair wrong. The generator certifies each pair's idea label, and the number of pairs per idea is fixed below. This is how each idea is shown to be needed.
  28: 8. **Checker:** every Phase B and C answer is certified by [landing-square-certificate.mjs](../../../.codebase/src/search-and-feedback/landing-square-certificate.mjs) (built 2026-11-03, 9 tests). For any legal non-king move it lists every legal opponent capture of the moved piece on its landing square, the attackers that are pinned, and the exchange values; `combinedAnswer` applies the combined rule below. It must pass an independent audit and the feedback quality gate before a phase starts.
  29: 9. **Generators** (programs that pick twin pairs from training-pool games) must exist and pass audit before each phase starts. No generator exists yet for any phase, including Phase A; the restart plan's rebuild list (section 11, item 6) still lists the new puzzle generator as to be built.
 110: ### C1: ideas 1 and 2
 111: 
 112: **Locked problems:** 60 twin pairs from 60 unrelated games. Every board is a capture that would lose material if recaptured, and a pinned enemy piece attacks the target square.
 113: 
 114: - 30 **pin pairs:** the only attacker of the target is pinned (good) or the pin line is blocked so it is not pinned (bad).
 115: - 30 **recapture pairs:** the pinned attacker is present on both twins; an extra unpinned attacker is absent (good) or present (bad).
 116: 
 117: **Practice:** 480 trials of idea 1 and 480 trials of idea 2 [choice]. Order is idea 1 then 2 in run 1, idea 2 then 1 in run 2, and alternating blocks of 96 in run 3.
 118: 
 119: **Arms:** Full fly (ideas 1 and 2); idea-1-only fly (480 filler trials in place of idea 2); idea-2-only fly (filler in place of idea 1); fresh fly (960 filler trials); shuffled-feedback fly; plasticity-off fly; simple learner; similar-board recall; exact-recall store; and the **fixed-sum combiner**, which adds the idea-1-only fly's and idea-2-only fly's move scores with a fixed rule. The fixed-sum combiner tests the review's warning that a hand-written rule could combine two answers without the fly learning to combine.
 120: 
 121: **Pass scores (all three runs):**
 122: 
 123: 1. **C1-a:** Full fly at least 44/60.
 124: 2. **C1-b:** at least 22/30 on pin pairs and at least 22/30 on recapture pairs.
 125: 3. **C1-c:** at least 6 more pairs than every other arm, including the best single-idea fly and the fixed-sum combiner, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0.
 126: 4. **C1-d:** retention: each single idea's development set falls by at most 10%.
 127: 5. **C1-e:** independent audit, as B6.
 128: 
 129: **Before C1 runs:** a software check on made-up data must show that the frozen move score can express all four required answer patterns (both idea conditions on and off) without a selector. If it cannot, stop and report it as a limit of the score, not a failure of the fly.
 130: 
 131: ### C2: ideas 1, 2 and 3
 132: 
 133: **Locked problems:** 60 twin pairs from 60 unrelated games. Every board is a capture where a pinned enemy piece attacks the target square and the exchange value matters.
 134: 
 135: - 20 **pin pairs:** captured value lower than the capturing piece; the only attacker is pinned (good) or not pinned (bad).
 136: - 20 **recapture pairs:** captured value lower; the pinned attacker is on both twins; an unpinned attacker is absent (good) or present (bad).
 137: - 20 **value pairs:** an unpinned attacker is on both twins; the captured piece is worth at least the capturing piece (good) or less (bad).
 138: 
 139: **Practice:** 480 trials of each idea [choice], with three counterbalanced orders across the three runs (1-2-3, 3-1-2, and alternating blocks of 96).
 140: 
 141: **Arms:** Full fly; each two-idea fly (filler in place of the missing idea); each single-idea fly; fresh fly; shuffled-feedback fly; plasticity-off fly; simple learner; similar-board recall; exact-recall store; fixed-sum combiner of the three single-idea flies.
 142: 
 143: **Pass scores (all three runs):**
 144: 
 145: 1. **C2-a:** Full fly at least 44/60.
 146: 2. **C2-b:** at least 15/20 on each of the three pair groups. (At random, the chance for one group is 2.1%.)
 147: 3. **C2-c:** at least 6 more pairs than every other arm, including the best two-idea fly and the fixed-sum combiner, each with bootstrap lower limit above 0.
 148: 4. **C2-d and C2-e:** retention and audit, as in C1.
 149: 
 150: ### Stopping rules for C1 and C2
 151: 
 152: 1. C1 does not start unless Phases A and B passed. C2 does not start unless C1 passed.
 153: 2. The checker audit, the generators and the score-expression check must pass first.
 154: 3. Training stops at the fixed trial counts. Snapshot choice and early stops follow the Phase B rules.
 155: 4. Each locked set is opened once. No pass score is lowered after data.
 156: 
 157: ### Results
 158: 
 159: - C1 passes: the fly combined two separately learned chess ideas on unfamiliar problems in this model. This is not yet a reasoning claim.
 160: - C2 passes, with Phases A, B, C1 and every audit passed: the first evidence for reasoning under the researcher's definition, inside chess and inside this model. Only then may "EUREKA EUREKA" be used.
 161: - Full fly no better than its best fewer-idea arm: no combination.
 162: - Full fly no better than the fixed-sum combiner: the combination can be explained by a fixed addition of separate scores.

codex
The five original findings are resolved at the design level. Three new gaps remain: moved-timing replays change the state entering the next block; section 8 still uses the old forgetting reference; and repeated-input removal can leave the quarter-capacity diary holding every distinct experience.

