**VERDICT: APPROVE WITH REQUIRED CHANGES — building allowed after the required-BUILD corrections below.**

Reviewed revision 7; blob `8d1fc4f2` matches.

**Round-1 findings**

1. **RESOLVED:** full-history recall and nonlinear-feature controls added; attribution explicitly excludes untested classifiers.
2. **RESOLVED:** offset-cancelled rankings and an all-legal choice gate are required.
3. **RESOLVED:** failed mixed-order training establishes schedule failure; a capacity limit needs separate proof.
4. **RESOLVED:** assisted retention belongs to Volume 3; Properties 1 and 2 remain unmet.
5. **RESOLVED:** removal is this candidate’s restriction. The one-page interface introduces no direct scoring operation.

**New findings**

1. **required-BUILD — The claimed matching of starting states is false for moved timing.**  
   **Evidence:** moved-timing replays occur after the earlier snapshot but before the next block ([design:271](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:271)); nevertheless, all five replay controls supposedly start the next block in the diary’s state ([design:331](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:331)). The no-old-replays arm also cannot remain identical at the start of *every* later block after earlier interventions ([design:269](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:269)).  
   **Consequence:** implementations could disagree about resets, or describe earlier strengthening as matched initial learning.  
   **Smallest fix:** distinguish equal snapshots from equal block-entry states. Explicitly exempt moved timing; describe later differences as accumulated intervention effects. Do not reset them away.

2. **required-BUILD — The stopping rules retain the superseded forgetting reference.**  
   **Evidence:** stage 2 uses **no old replays** to establish forgetting ([design:319](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:319)); section 8 still stops when **no diary** retains the lesson ([design:359](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:359)).  
   **Consequence:** one outcome can satisfy the stage prerequisite while triggering “nothing to repair.”  
   **Smallest fix:** make section 8 use the same stage-specific no-old-replays prerequisite.

3. **required-LAUNCH — Quarter capacity does not guarantee storage pressure after duplicate removal.**  
   **Evidence:** capacity is based on lesson count, while identical inputs replace earlier entries ([design:186](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:186), [design:191](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:191)). Stage 1 has at most eight distinct inputs, repeatedly presented ([design:289](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:289)).  
   **Consequence:** the diary can retain every distinct experience while being described as operating under pressure.  
   **Smallest fix:** label stage 1 a retention assay without storage pressure. Before chess launch, require distinct-input counts exceeding capacity and report genuine evictions separately from supersessions.

4. **optional — The one-page loop omits replay publication.**  
   **Evidence:** its loop logs the replay and continues ([design:81](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:81)); the detailed contract requires an atomic replay publication ([design:202](C:/Users/ethan/Documents/fly-chess-research/documentation/design/neural-learning/replay-diary-memory-design-2026-11-17.md:202)).  
   **Consequence:** following the summary alone gives different recovery behaviour.  
   **Smallest fix:** add the required publication step to the loop.

**Strongest supportable sentence, conditional on passing:** “Replay assisted retention and improved verified-good choices on locked three-idea chess tests beyond the specified case-recall and simple-learner controls, with the diary absent during scoring; an untested case classifier remains possible.”
